TEXT 160 0 Left 3 ;#100 Capacitor self-resonance, measured with a generator and a scope
TEXT 160 64 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C3wYMZ_cjdQ
TEXT 160 112 Left 2 ;A capacitor is really a capacitor, its lead inductance and its loss resistance in series. The
TEXT 160 144 Left 2 ;generator drives it through a 10 dB pad and the scope watches the voltage across it.
TEXT 1330 336 Left 2 !.ac dec 200 10k 100Meg
TEXT 1330 376 Left 2 !.meas ac VOUT_LF FIND mag(V(out)) AT 100k
TEXT 1330 416 Left 2 !.meas ac VOUT_MIN MIN mag(V(out))
TEXT 1330 456 Left 2 !.meas ac VOUT_SR FIND mag(V(out)) AT 3.262Meg
TEXT 1330 496 Left 2 !.meas ac VOUT_HF FIND mag(V(out)) AT 30Meg
TEXT 1330 536 Left 2 !.meas ac ZDUT_SR FIND mag(V(out)/I(Cdut)) AT 3.262Meg
TEXT 160 800 Left 2 ;Alan's fixture and his 103 capacitor are what the video shows: a 5 Vpp generator set for a high-impedance
TEXT 160 832 Left 2 ;load, a 10 dB pad, and a 10x probe watching the voltage across the part. Cdut is his 10 nF.
TEXT 160 880 Left 2 ;Lesl is 238 nH, which puts self-resonance at 3.262 MHz, within 0.1 percent of the 3.26506 MHz his scope reads.
TEXT 160 912 Left 2 ;Resr is an assumption: he never measures it, and 2 ohm puts the depth of the notch where he sees it.
